  "bodyText": "A 29-year-old fictional female patient with rheumatoid arthritis began subcutaneous Zolapamine 50 mg weekly. Five days after her third injection, she noticed a 4 cm area of erythema, warmth, and mild swelling at the injection site on her thigh. She reported no fever or systemic symptoms. On examination, the site showed well-demarcated erythema without fluctuance or drainage, consistent with a delayed local hypersensitivity reaction rather than infection. She was treated with a topical corticosteroid and instructed to rotate injection sites more consistently. The reaction resolved over 10 days, and she continued Zolapamine therapy without recurrence at the six-month follow-up. This describes a specific adverse drug reaction to a specific pharmaceutical product in an identifiable patient case.",
